  "id": "Compound::DB00514 - Gene::1565",
  "verbalization": "Dextromethorphan, a common cough suppressant, is shown to bind to Cyp2b6 and Cyp2e1, two isoforms of the cytochrome P450 2 family. These enzymes are involved in the metabolism of arachidonic acid, a key precursor in the production of pro-inflammatory eicosanoids. The observed relationship between Dextromethorphan and Cyp2d6 is mediated through the arachidonic acid monooxygenase and epoxygenase pathways, which are both catalyzed by Cyp2b6 and Cyp2e1, respectively. The shared involvement of these enzymes in the metabolism of arachidonic acid suggests a potential mechanism by which Dextromethorphan may influence lipid metabolism and inflammatory responses. The consistent binding and participation scores across the three paths indicate a robust, biologically plausible link between Dextromethorphan and Cyp2d6, supported by the shared functional role of these enzymes in arachidonic acid metabolism. The evidence suggests that Dextromethorphan may modulate Cyp2d6 activity, potentially affecting the metabolic pathways that regulate inflammation and lipid homeostasis.",
